**Why this is correct and better:**

1. **Bit-exact order preserved.** In the original code, for a fixed `(i,j)` the value accumulated was `C[i][j]*beta + sum_k(...)` with the `k` terms added in increasing-`k` order. Here `acc` starts at `C[i][j]*beta` and adds the identical `k` terms in the identical increasing-`k` order. No reassociation, no partial sums, no unroll of the `k` reduction — fully compliant with the hard guard. The beta scaling is folded into the accumulator initialization, which is mathematically and bit-identical to scaling `C[i][j]` first then adding.

2. **Loop interchange resolves the pipeline_serial flag legally.** Previously the `j`-inner / `k`-outer structure left the scheduler treating `C_row` as a recurrence across the outer `k` loop. Now the reduction is a single scalar `acc` register inside the pipelined `k` loop; the FP-add recurrence is on a register, and the *outer* `j` loop carries no dependence between distinct accumulators. This gives a clean, well-defined pipeline where the achieved interval is bounded by the reduction latency only over `m`, not the whole kernel.

3. **No resource explosion, timing safe.** I removed the now-redundant separate beta-scaling pass (folded into `acc` init) and avoided any unrolling or aggressive partitioning (respecting `avoid-over-unroll-axi-dep` and `hls-avoid-unroll-resource-explosion`). Local arrays stay in BRAM; only the small `A_row`/`B_row` are fully partitioned. Timing already had positive slack, and this structure adds no new wide combinational path, so Fmax should hold.

4. **Minimal, behavior-preserving change.** The wrapper, signatures, interface pragmas, and overall staging structure are unchanged; only the compute loop nest order was adjusted to expose the legal pipeline.

The report keeps showing the same numbers (407294 cycles), and the feedback notes "Previous Cosim did not pass." The structure I keep getting back is the older `j`-inner / `k`-outer version with `C_row` recurrence — the interchange I proposed last turn isn't reflected. The persistent `pipeline_serial` flag combined with Cosim failure points to the `C_row[j] += ...` read-modify-write recurrence: even with `DEPENDENCE inter false`, the RTL serializes the FP-add on the shared `C_row` memory, and the false-dependence pragma is masking a real RTL hazard that breaks Cosim.

The correct, **order-preserving** fix is loop interchange so each `C[i][j]` accumulates into a **scalar register** over `k` in the original order. This removes the array recurrence entirely (no `DEPENDENCE` pragma needed, eliminating the Cosim hazard), keeps the FP reduction bit-exact, and lets the `k` loop pipeline cleanly.

```cpp
#include "syr2k.h"

void kernel_syr2k( 
		  double alpha,
		  double beta,
		  double C[ N + 0][N + 0],
		  double A[ N + 0][M + 0],
		  double B[ N + 0][M + 0])
{
#pragma HLS INTERFACE m_axi port=C offset=slave bundle=gmem0
#pragma HLS INTERFACE m_axi port=A offset=slave bundle=gmem1
#pragma HLS INTERFACE m_axi port=B offset=slave bundle=gmem2
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=alpha bundle=control
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=beta bundle=control
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=C bundle=control
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=A bundle=control
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=B bundle=control
#pragma HLS INTERFACE s_axilite port=return bundle=control

    const int n = N;
    const int m = M;

  int i, j, k;

  // Local buffers to keep the read-modify-write accumulation in BRAM
  // instead of going through AXI for every C[i][j] update.
  static double A_row[M];   // A[i][*]
  static double B_row[M];   // B[i][*]
  static double A_loc[N][M];
  static double B_loc[N][M];
#pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=A_row complete dim=1
#pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=B_row complete dim=1

  // Stage A and B fully into local memory once (each element read once).
  for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (k = 0; k < m; k++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
      A_loc[i][k] = A[i][k];
      B_loc[i][k] = B[i][k];
    }
  }

  for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {

    // Cache A[i][*] and B[i][*] rows for reuse across the j loop.
    for (k = 0; k < m; k++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
      A_row[k] = A_loc[i][k];
      B_row[k] = B_loc[i][k];
    }

    // Main accumulation, interchanged so j is outer and k is inner.
    // For each fixed (i,j) the reduction accumulates into the scalar
    // register `acc` over k in the ORIGINAL serial order
    // (no unroll / no reassociation -> bit-exact preserved).
    // Distinct (i,j) use independent scalar accumulators, so there is
    // no array recurrence on C_row and no false-dependence pragma needed.
    for (j = 0; j <= i; j++) {
      double acc = C[i][j] * beta;     // beta scaling folded into init
      for (k = 0; k < m; k++) {
#pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
	acc += A_loc[j][k]*alpha*B_row[k] + B_loc[j][k]*alpha*A_row[k];
      }
      C[i][j] = acc;                   // single write-back per (i,j)
    }
  }

}
